  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 330 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nWarming trend through next week, topping out near 70 F:\n\nOnce the aforementioned trough slides to our south and\nsoutheast, global ensembles depict the center of a pronounced \nridge axis overspreading our region. This will act to warm\ntemperatures back to above normal through next week. High\ntemperatures are expected to begin in the middle to upper 50s on\nMonday, topping out around 70 F by Friday. Lows are also\nforecast to escape the twenties, starting in the 30s Tuesday,\nand increasing to the upper 40s to middle 50s by Friday night.\n\nThe NBM temperature spreads on this warming trend are very small\nand narrow, lending high confidence in the temperature forecast.\n\n\nTiming uncertainties in next system, earliest arrival Thursday:\n\nConfidence begins to breakdown as the trough on the other side\nof the pronounced ridge shifts its way toward our region at the\nend of the work week. Ensemble cluster analysis depicts very\nlarge timing differences in the longwave pattern, with greater\ndifferences in shortwave evolutions embedded within the flow. \nDepending on how quickly the longwave trough pushes east, a \nsystem may bring rain to the area Thursday and Friday. Though \nagain, timing differences lead to only a 15-25% chance of rain \nas up to 75% of ensemble members keep us completely dry.\n\nInteresting to note with this system, the deterministic GFS\nkeeps us dry despite model parameters suggesting synoptic lift\nwithin a ribbon of sufficient moisture over our area. It's hard\nto believe no rain would come of that. The ENS and AIFS have \nbeen a bit more bullish on at least a bit of rain with any\nsystem during the Thursday and Friday timeframe.\n\nEither way, the mean and interquartile solution places the\nlongwave axis being close enough to our area by next weekend for\nbetter rain chances. But at this point, the exact placement,\nshape, and amplitude of any shortwaves are highly erratic in\nensemble members, owing very little confidence in anything more\nthan a pattern beginning to favor rainfall. Indeed, the CPC\nplaces us in a 50-60% chance for above normal precipitation\nbetween November 19-23 as that longwave inches closer to us,\nplacing us under southwesterly flow and associated moisture\nadvection. Trends will continue to be monitored on what that\nexactly means for the Ozarks.",
